  "market_commentary": {
    "overview": "",
    "bulkers": "The Baltic Dry Index (BDI) closed today at 2,660 points, up 149 points or almost 6% on last Friday's \n2,511 points, bringing a run of four consecutive weekly declines to an end. The recovery was led by the \nCapesizes, with the BCI gaining almost 17% w-o-w to 3,791 points, although the index eased slightly on the \nday. The Panamaxes remained under pressure, with the BPI losing a further 9% w-o-w to 2,109 points, while \nthe geared sizes held their ground, with the BSI edging up to 1,887 points and the BHSI steady at 1,020 points, \nits highest level this year: \nBALTIC INDICES \nPOINTS \nCHANGE \nBaltic Dry Index \n2660 \n\u25bc -33 \nBaltic Capesize Index\n3791\n\u25bc-57\nBaltic Panamax Index\n2109\n\u25bc-42\nBaltic Supramax Index \n1887 \n\u25bc -3 \nBaltic Handysize Index \n1020 \n\u25ac 0 \n \nSale & Purchase: Second-hand activity eased slightly compared with recent weeks, but values held firm in \nboth the dry and wet sectors. In dry bulk, buying interest remained steady across all sizes with a clear tilt \ntowards the smaller geared segments, where a broader choice of tonnage makes deals easier to conclude, \nwhile Japanese-built vessels continued to attract strong demand: the oh/box shaped handysize MOUNTPARK \n(38k blt 2016 Naikai) fetched $ 23.5 mill and the supramax YASA UNSAL SUNAR (55k blt 2007/Mitsui) \n$ 15.1 mill. In the larger sizes, the post-panamax MARITIME KING (96k blt 2011 Imabari) changed hands for \nregion $ 19 mill basis forward delivery January 2027. In total, 11 bulk carrier sales were recorded, comprising \n1 post-panamax, 2 ultramaxes, 4 supramaxes and 4 handysize vessels. The tanker market stayed very active \nand firm, with prompt tonnage commanding a premium and Middle Eastern buyers paying top prices: the \nSuezmax ZOUZOU N. achieved $ 103.3 mill, above newbuilding parity, the VLCC VADIN fetched $ 117 mill \nbasis prompt delivery Fujairah, almost double the price vessel was sold ($60 mill) just seven months ago in \nApril 2026. In containers, buying interest continues to exceed the available tonnage, keeping values at \nelevated levels. \nFor a detailed review and additional information regarding this week's transactions, kindly refer to our \n\"representative sales\" section.",
    "demolition": "Recycling activity remained thin, with only a handful of candidates circulating, mostly from the \ndry segment, as firm freight markets keep older tonnage trading; in Pakistan, two dry bulk demolition sales \n\n  L I O N S H I P B R O K E R S \nW E E K L Y M A R K E T R E P O R T \n\u00a9 Lion 2 \nwere reportedly called off after sellers received better offers from trading buyers. India remains firm with \nincreased demand for specialized vessels, while Bangladesh remains the most competitive destination amid \na tight supply of tonnage, as reflected by the FOTEX (Ldt 2600 tweendecker) at $ 530 per LT and the LIAN YI \nDA 2 HAO (Ldt 5560 handy bulker) at $ 512 per LT. Appetite in Pakistan has cooled due to subdued steel \ndemand and renewed steel imports from Iran, while Turkey held steady despite slowing local steel demand. \nLooking ahead, possible sanctions relief for certain tankers could bring additional candidates to the market. \nFor an overview of demolition market rates, please check our comprehensive demometer below:"
  },
